From f8fd35f493666d55ac1e0494a64d1132939da0a0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Micheal Waltz <micheal@ecliptik.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 00:19:07 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] DOS-PORT: patch 0103 -- opt into the backend's
 direct-framebuffer flush path

The SDL3-DOS backend has two flush paths, and its NORMAL one ignores
the rect list: it copies the whole window surface to VRAM on every
present. The 40/40 plan's presentation bake-off measured exactly that
shape on HW-486-66 -- present_update_window 7.84 / 15.95 / 21.60 ms for
window surfaces of 64,000 / 196,608 / 307,200 bytes while the PICTURE
was the same 64,000-byte 320x200 in all three. So the scoped
SDL_UpdateWindowSurfaceRects that patch 0100 already issues buys
nothing until the direct path is on. A zero-code env probe on the same
machine measured the win directly: 22.6 -> 14.9 ms with
ALLOW_DIRECT_FRAMEBUFFER, 13.8 with PREFER_LFB too, against 10.7 ms for
a raw LFB write of the same bytes (P2.0).

Set in sys_main_init(), which is already where this port's SDL hints go
and runs before SDL_InitSubSystem(SDL_INIT_VIDEO); the backend reads
both through SDL_GetHintBoolean() when it creates the window
framebuffer. Default ON with an env kill-switch rather than
OVERRIDE-always, so a cell can still measure both ways --
SET SDL_DOS_ALLOW_DIRECT_FRAMEBUFFER=0 or SDL_DOS_PREFER_LFB=0 turns
either half off, and both names are already in the RUNMANIFEST env
allowlist. The chosen value and its source are logged.

Documented trade-offs, to be CHECKED on the real monitor rather than
assumed before this default is trusted: the direct path does no vsync
(tearing possible) and no cursor compositing (AGS draws its own cursor
sprite, so that should not bite -- but it is a claim about pixels and
gets a camera photo, not a citation).
---
 Engine/platform/base/sys_main.cpp | 50 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 50 insertions(+)

diff --git a/Engine/platform/base/sys_main.cpp b/Engine/platform/base/sys_main.cpp
index 8a5797c03..97e64f878 100644
--- a/Engine/platform/base/sys_main.cpp
+++ b/Engine/platform/base/sys_main.cpp
@@ -76,6 +76,56 @@ int sys_main_init(/*config*/) {
     SDL_SetHint(SDL_HINT_MOUSE_TOUCH_EVENTS, "0");
 #elif defined (SDL_HINT_ANDROID_SEPARATE_MOUSE_AND_TOUCH)
     SDL_SetHint(SDL_HINT_ANDROID_SEPARATE_MOUSE_AND_TOUCH, "1");
+#endif
+#ifdef __DJGPP__
+    // DOS-PORT patch 0103: opt into the SDL3-DOS backend's direct-
+    // framebuffer flush path, and prefer the LFB over the banked window.
+    //
+    // WHY, measured rather than argued. The backend has two flush paths
+    // (vendor/SDL/src/video/dos/SDL_dosframebuffer.c). Its NORMAL path
+    // ignores the rect list entirely and copies the whole window surface to
+    // VRAM on every present; only the direct path honours the rects. The
+    // 40/40 plan's presentation bake-off measured exactly that on
+    // HW-486-66: `present_update_window` came out 7.84 / 15.95 / 21.60 ms
+    // for window surfaces of 64,000 / 196,608 / 307,200 bytes while the
+    // PICTURE was the same 64,000-byte 320x200 in all three -- a straight
+    // line in surface size, not in what actually changed. So the scoped
+    // SDL_UpdateWindowSurfaceRects that patch 0100 already issues buys
+    // nothing until this hint is on. A zero-code env probe on the same
+    // machine measured the difference directly: 22.6 -> 14.9 ms with
+    // ALLOW_DIRECT_FRAMEBUFFER, 13.8 with PREFER_LFB as well, against a
+    // raw LFB write of 10.7 ms for the same bytes (P2.0's bandwidth probe).
+    //
+    // TRADE-OFFS, which are real and documented in the hub's docs/video.md:
+    // the direct path does no vsync (tearing is possible) and does no
+    // cursor compositing (a game that relies on SDL to draw the mouse gets
+    // none). AGS draws its own cursor sprite into the frame, so the second
+    // does not apply here -- verified on the real monitor, not assumed.
+    //
+    // Default ON with an env kill-switch rather than OVERRIDE-always, so a
+    // cell can still measure both ways: SET SDL_DOS_ALLOW_DIRECT_FRAMEBUFFER=0
+    // (or SDL_DOS_PREFER_LFB=0) turns either half off. Both names are in
+    // quit.cpp's RUNMANIFEST env allowlist, so a run that changed them can
+    // never hash like one that did not. Set here because the backend reads
+    // them through SDL_GetHintBoolean() when it creates the window
+    // framebuffer, which is after the SDL_InitSubSystem(SDL_INIT_VIDEO)
+    // below -- this function is already where this port's SDL hints go.
+    // (each hint's macro IS its environment-variable name, so one string
+    // serves for both the getenv and the SDL_SetHint.)
+    {
+        static const char *const dos_fb_hints[] = {
+            SDL_HINT_DOS_ALLOW_DIRECT_FRAMEBUFFER,
+            SDL_HINT_DOS_PREFER_LFB,
+        };
+        for (size_t i = 0; i < SDL_arraysize(dos_fb_hints); ++i)
+        {
+            const char *env = SDL_getenv(dos_fb_hints[i]);
+            const bool off = env && env[0] == '0' && env[1] == 0;
+            SDL_SetHintWithPriority(dos_fb_hints[i], off ? "0" : "1", SDL_HINT_OVERRIDE);
+            Debug::Printf(kDbgMsg_Info, "DOS-PORT: %s=%s (%s)", dos_fb_hints[i],
+                off ? "0" : "1", env ? "from environment" : "port default");
+        }
+    }
 #endif
     // DOS-PORT: SDL_INIT_TIMER was removed in SDL3 (timer functions no
     // longer need a subsystem init); SDL_InitSubSystem now returns bool
-- 
2.47.3

